  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe primary focus during the extended forecast period is a robust \nupper-level trough impacting at least the eastern half of the CONUS \nduring the latter half of next week. Prior to that, guidance \nconsensus remains that weak, broad upper-level ridging will continue \nto build into the Midwest as low-level flow becomes increasingly \nsouthwesterly. This will boost temperatures on Monday compared to \nSunday. Thanks to a warmer start to the day Tuesday and continued \nlow-level warm air advection, Tuesday will be even warmer, with \nglobal ensemble members tightly focused on highs around 70 degrees. \n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned upper-level trough will begin \ndigging equatorward. The majority of guidance continues to trend \ntoward a solution that supports a more aggressive and westward \ntrough. This has led to decreasing spread among ensemble members for \nour temperatures toward the end of the week, with confidence \nincreasing in at least a brief period of below-normal temperatures. \n\nDespite this increasing consensus, the phasing of the trough and \nmore so the embedded shortwaves has and continues to vary greatly \nrun-to-run and source-to-source. This will dictate specifics on how \nquickly we'll cool, precipitation chances with at least an initial \nFROPA, and how quickly temperatures moderate afterwards. If we do \nsee precipitation with this trough, Wednesday-Thursday is when \nconfidence is highest it would occur. Despite uncertainty in how \nquickly temperatures cool mid-week and rebound later in the weekend, \nguidance consensus has consistently shown Friday to be the coolest \nday of the period.",
